<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  naudio — launchd AGENT for na_audio_daemon (issue #96 item 2).
  SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later

  Generated by CMake from this template: the program path and log destination follow
  CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX, so the unit is truthful for the package that carries it rather
  than for the machine that built it.

  An AGENT (per-user, gui/<uid> domain), deliberately NOT a LaunchDaemon (root, system
  domain). A root daemon has no TCC context, so macOS can never grant it microphone
  access — it would install cleanly, start cleanly, and then fail to capture with no
  way for the operator to fix it. The per-user agent is also what makes the config file
  at ~/Library/Application Support/naudio/daemon.conf (issue #96 item 1) the right file
  for this service to read.

  There is deliberately NO Disabled key (issue #102). Installing a package must not start
  capturing a microphone at the next login, and that is a property of the ARGUMENTS below
  (control mode, autostart off: the page is served, no device is opened), not of a key
  that stops the job from running at all. Until rc8 the key was there, and it produced the
  bug the issue reports: Login Items showed the agent switched ON while launchd had never
  bootstrapped it, so the operator saw two switches that disagreed. Measured 2026-09-13:
  the Login Items switch and `launchctl enable`/`disable` are independent stores in both
  directions — the switch boots the job out or in and writes no launchd override, and an
  override moves nothing in the pane — so the only way to have ONE switch is for Login
  Items to be it and for this file not to fight it. An rc7-era `launchctl disable`
  override would still block bootstrap while the switch said on; the postinstall clears
  it for the console user. The servicecheck arm asserts the key is absent AND runs these
  arguments to prove they capture nothing; the release gate does the same on the
  installed files.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.kj5hst.naudio.daemon</string>

    <!-- The run time is pinned to 0 HERE, on the command line, rather than in a
         shipped config file: a service runs until it is stopped, always, and a flag
         cannot be undone by a stray file (flags beat the file, issue #96 item 1).
         Every other setting comes from the config file.

         The mode is control (issue #96 item 4), which is a strict superset of what
         this agent ran before. Control mode serves the localhost page and captures
         NOTHING until something asks it to, so logging in with the agent enabled
         gives the operator a page to click rather than a microphone that is already
         open. An operator who wants capture to begin at login sets autostart = true
         in the config file, which the page writes from a checkbox; the choice is
         theirs to make rather than the package's to assume.

         NB: no literal double hyphen may appear in these comments. XML forbids it,
         and while launchd and plutil both accept it anyway (measured), every
         conformant parser rejects the file outright. -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>@NAUDIO_SERVICE_EXEC@</string>
        <string>--mode</string>
        <string>control</string>
        <string>--duration-ms</string>
        <string>0</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- What Login Items calls this agent (issue #102). Background Task Management groups a
         launchd job under the developer name on its executable's signature unless the job
         names an app here; then it is shown under that app's name, so the switch reads
         "Network Audio Service" rather than the person who signed the daemon. Honoured ONLY
         when the named bundle is Developer ID signed with the daemon's Team ID (measured
         2026-09-13: the key was silently dropped for an unsigned app, with no diagnostic
         anywhere), which is why cmake/CodesignPayload.cmake signs Network Audio Service.app
         and the servicecheck arm asserts this value is the bundle's CFBundleIdentifier. -->
    <key>AssociatedBundleIdentifiers</key>
    <array>
        <string>org.kj5hst.naudio.control</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Restart on any unsuccessful exit, throttled. launchd cannot discriminate exit
         CODES the way systemd's RestartPreventExitStatus= can, so unlike the systemd
         unit this also retries a permanent config error (exit 2) every 30 s. That is
         why the log destination below is not optional: the file:line refusal is the
         only way an operator sees which line of daemon.conf is wrong. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <dict>
        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>30</integer>

    <!-- launchd does not expand ~ or $HOME, so a per-user log path cannot be expressed
         here; the postinstall creates this directory group-writable by staff (every
         local macOS account is in staff) so the agent can write as the logged-in user.
         systemd needs no equivalent — journald captures the unit's stdout/stderr.
         This directory is load-bearing, not merely diagnostic: if it does not exist,
         launchd cannot open the log and the job silently never starts (measured). Do
         not "simplify" the postinstall by dropping the mkdir. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>@NAUDIO_SERVICE_LOG@</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>@NAUDIO_SERVICE_LOG@</string>
</dict>
</plist>
